Expected elements: present

Identity bar with hash, block 63675, exit to `aztec-testnet`, stepping groups, `128 / 345` and the phase rail; no band above it; `DATA` + `Real Aztec testnet data` + the capture's sentences as the Transaction pane's first row; all three source-less panes explained in their own words; `Supply sources` present; no invented source, no fixture content, no engine-failure banner.

The removed band is a real gain — the panes now start 68px from the top and the identity bar reads as the only chrome. But the space it bought went to the panes that had nothing to put in it. The Code pane is roughly 910×1000px holding two sentences and one button in its top 280px, the Call Trace pane holds two lines above 500px of white, and the Transaction pane — the only region with content — is the narrowest at ~390px and is the one thing on the page that overflows, clipped mid-line at `"bodyRetainedAtCapture": true,` by the viewport edge. That inversion is the page's layout problem: 1500px of width is allocated to emptiness while the pane carrying the provenance claim, the fee, the executions and the raw payload is squeezed into a gutter and truncated. Within that gutter the DATA paragraph and the three-line fee both break the label/value grid, aligning to nothing.

Highest-priority fixes: rebalance the split toward the Transaction pane (or reflow its rows into the empty Code pane width); give the DATA paragraph its own full-width block below the label rather than the value gutter.

Rating: 6/10

